From ana@example.org Mon Mar  4 09:00:00 2024
From: Ana Writer <ana@example.org>
To: friend@example.net
Subject: On keeping a notebook
Date: Mon, 4 Mar 2024 09:00:00 +0000
Message-ID: <m1@example.org>
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=utf-8

I kept a notebook for eleven years before I understood what it was for. It was not for remembering, though I told myself that; the entries I reread are the ones I already remember, and the ones I have forgotten I do not go looking for. It was for slowing down. A sentence written by hand takes as long as it takes, and in that time the thought has to hold still and be looked at, which most thoughts cannot survive. The notebook was a sieve. What came through it was smaller than what went in, and better, and mine in a way that the quick things were not.

People ask how to start one and I tell them the truth, which is that the first three are bad and you throw them away. Not literally: you keep them, and once a decade you open one and wince. But you throw away the idea that they were the thing. The thing starts when you stop performing for the reader you imagine looking over your shoulder, and that takes about three notebooks of practice, and nobody I know has skipped it. The hand gets tired before the performance does, which is the mercy of the medium.

On Sun, 3 Mar 2024 at 21:10, Friend <friend@example.net> wrote:
> Do you still keep the notebook?
> I stopped.

-- 
Ana
sent from a desk

